# Line-ending policy for the nyairo repository.
#
# Why this file exists: the shipped source manifest (MANIFEST.json) records the
# SHA-256 of every file's raw bytes, and scripts/verify_manifest.py re-hashes the
# working tree. Without an explicit policy the bytes a clone receives depend on
# the cloner's core.autocrlf: Git for Windows defaults to `true`, which turned
# 1,527 text files into CRLF on checkout and made the source verifier report
# 1,510 mismatches (rc=1). A single repo-wide rule makes every supported
# checkout byte-identical, so the verifier passes on Windows and Linux alike.
#
# `text=auto` still lets Git auto-detect binary content; eol=lf makes the
# working tree match the index on every platform (no phantom whole-file diffs).
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell is the deliberate CRLF exception: PS 5.1 tooling expects CRLF.
# vendor/hermes/.gitattributes already declares this for its own subtree (deeper
# attribute files win, so that stays authoritative); repeated here so the
# exception is explicit at the repository root. The 12 .ps1 files in the tree are
# already pinned as CRLF in MANIFEST.json, so this rule matches the manifest.
#
# .cmd/.bat are deliberately NOT given eol=crlf. The only such file in the tree is
# upstream's vendor/hermes/scripts/install.cmd: upstream stores it with LF and
# MANIFEST.json pins those LF bytes, so forcing CRLF on checkout both deviates
# from upstream and makes scripts/verify_manifest.py fail on every platform.
# If a first-party .cmd/.bat is ever added and genuinely needs CRLF, add a
# targeted path rule and regenerate MANIFEST.json in the same change.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Text types this project ships, pinned to LF explicitly.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.cjs     text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.svg     text eol=lf
*.patch   text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.sha256  text eol=lf

# Binary types present in the tree, plus the archive/database artifact types
# this project produces (.zip release packages, runtime .sqlite/.db stores).
# Never apply eol conversion to these.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
*.ico     binary
*.icns    binary
*.pdf     binary
*.woff2   binary
*.tflite  binary
*.onnx    binary
*.mp4     binary
*.wav     binary
*.zip     binary
*.sqlite  binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.db      binary
